1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,042
A fan is running. A damper has acknowledged its command. Can the shift
supervisor close the ventilation incident?

2
00:00:07,042 --> 00:00:16,750
A Korean labor ministry inspection called for better exhaust performance in a
school kitchen. Each facility needs a way to check its own equipment between
inspections.

3
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:25,167
In this virtual cooking line, the site target is nine hundred cubic metres per
hour. The operations app requests a fully open damper.

4
00:00:25,167 --> 00:00:33,208
The controller acknowledges one hundred percent. That tells the operator the
command arrived. It still leaves a question about the air.

5
00:00:33,208 --> 00:00:42,292
The next airflow measurement is three hundred sixty cubic metres per hour. The
operator keeps the incident open and maintenance starts a general inspection.

6
00:00:42,292 --> 00:00:52,000
A separate sensor on the mechanical shaft reports thirty-five percent open. The
technician now has a reason to check actuator travel and position feedback
first.

7
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:01,583
Here is the live dashboard for that run. The work order keeps its original
identity. Its inspection priority changes while the airflow incident stays open.

8
00:01:01,583 --> 00:01:11,292
The source measurement, command and inspection are linked through actual oneM2M
resources. Restarting the maintenance app recovered the same order without
duplicating it.

9
00:01:11,292 --> 00:01:23,208
In a second run, the bridge discovered an actual MEC sensor-sharing service,
retrieved a separate provider’s shaft measurement, and returned it to the
facility. The same inspection took priority.

10
00:01:23,208 --> 00:01:33,917
At a learning facility, a manager can describe another trial. A hosted model
saved a new lounge scenario and read the result. Stale evidence created zero
device commands.

11
00:01:33,917 --> 00:01:44,917
Run openSoom on your own machine. Choose the zone, set the facility target, and
replay the decision with virtual equipment. The code, traces and tools are open
source.
